<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ningbo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mingzhou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ningbo, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period as Mingzhou, is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s east coast. Under the Song Dynasty it was one of the main ports, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, for maritime trade. Customs revenues from these ports were significant to the Song economy, and the city was a key link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ningbo.jpg" alt="Ningbo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nbfreeh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianyi_Square_2019-07_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
